<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="5">
<Content>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. BRANSON TIMESHARE RESORT - SALES FLOOR - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A carpeted room the color of weak tea. Twelve round tables. A banner: “PARADISE IS CLOSER THAN YOU THINK.”</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>GARY PRUITT (60s, windbreaker, a pen behind each ear) sits with his arms folded. DONNA PRUITT (60s) eats a complimentary danish with surgical focus.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>CHAD (30s, teeth) slides a brochure across the table.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CHAD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Folks, I'm going to be honest with you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to Donna)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He's going to be honest with us.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That's nice. Nobody's honest anymore.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CHAD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This offer — and I mean this — expires when you walk out that door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Gary checks his watch. He clicks one pen. Then the other.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Chad. Can I call you Chad?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CHAD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It's my name.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Chad, we've toured forty-one properties in nineteen states. We have walked out of every door you can imagine. Revolving doors. Sliding doors. A door in Gatlinburg that was painted on a wall.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That one was a fire exit.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Donna is suddenly at the coffee station, filling a thermos from the urn.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The vouchers, Chad.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A long beat. Chad's smile does not move but something behind it dies.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RESORT PARKING LOT - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Gary and Donna walk to a 2004 Buick LeSabre with a cruise voucher each, fanned like winning cards. The vouchers are blacked out for every date that matters.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>These are blacked out through 2031.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then we'll go in 2032.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>FLASHBACK - THE PRUITT GARAGE - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A wall of unopened boxes, floor to ceiling: blenders, a karaoke machine, six identical &quot;spa&quot; robes &amp; a canoe.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>McKINLEY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(muffled, from inside a box)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is somebody there?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>FADE TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. PRUITT KITCHEN - MORNING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Donna slides an AMALFI COAST brochure out of a cookbook, looks at it… and slides it back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action" Alignment="Center"><Text>THE END OF THE BEGINNING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ONTARIO HIGHWAY - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The Buick crawls along a gravel detour. A sign: TOLL BRIDGE 3 KM. Gary swerves onto a dirt road.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That was six dollars, Gary. Six.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It's the principle, Donna.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It's always the principle.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. NORTHERN PINES RESORT - LOBBY - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Empty. Echoing. KYLE (20s, sweat stains shaped like continents) waves them in with both arms.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KYLE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Welcome! Welcome to Northern Pines, where every season is &lt;the season&gt; &amp; every guest is &quot;family.&quot;</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He hands Gary a stack of contracts thick enough to stop a door. Gary signs each page without looking, the way other men breathe.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KYLE (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You don't — I mean you could read those. People read those.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody reads those, Kyle.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Donna looks up from her danish. She looks at Kyle. Kyle looks away first.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>SMASH CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RESORT LOADING DOCK - LATER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>An 85-INCH TELEVISION in its box leans against the Buick, which is roughly the same size as the box.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Gary and Donna stand with their hands on their hips, studying the problem like surveyors. This action line is long on purpose so that it wraps across several lines in the PDF and the Word file and the Final Draft file, and keeps going until it has to wrap at least three times to prove the wrapping code is right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It'll fit.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It will not fit.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everything fits if you believe in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. MOTEL ROOM - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The TV, unboxed, fills an entire wall. Its standby light pulses like a heartbeat. Gary sleeps. Donna does not.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At 3:00 A.M. the screen wakes by itself. A soft blue glow. Then text: HELLO, MR. PRUITT.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Gary.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(asleep)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Bonus feature.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The router on the dresser lights up like a pinball machine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FEDERAL BUILDING - INTERROGATION ROOM - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>AGENT HALVORSEN (50s, crew cut, a tie clip shaped like a tiny handcuff) spreads forty corporate filings across the table like a winning poker hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HALVORSEN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Pruitt Holdings. Pruitt Logistics. Pruitt Café &amp; Bakery — é, with the little hat. Very classy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GARY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I've never been to a café in my life.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HALVORSEN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(leaning in)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That's exactly what a mastermind would say.</Text></Paragraph>
</Content>
<TitlePage>
<Content>
<Paragraph Alignment="Center"><Text>LAST RESORT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Alignment="Center"><Text>Written by</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Alignment="Center"><Text>Dan Greene &amp; “Friends”</Text></Paragraph>
</Content>
